=== Psalm 13
--- Psalm 13 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 13
sub: Waiting for His perfect timing
refs: Psalm 13:1

--- How long wilt thou forget me?
kicker: Psalm 13 · How long?
scripture: Psalm 13:1-2 | mark: How long wilt thou forget me, O LORD?
p: Psalm 13 beautifully captures a very real feeling of the Christian life, the impatient waiting for God's perfect timing, turning into an acknowledgement that He has dealt bountifully with us.
p: It can be easy to slip into despair when facing trials. It can be easy for us as humans, weak in the flesh, to see the enemy seemingly triumph. To pray and wait upon the Lord, but feel like He may have forgotten you in regards to your trouble.

--- The Lord is not slack
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 13:1
p: We've discussed the idea that even as martyred saints in Heaven, this impatient call for the Lord to hurry and fulfill His judgment, occurs. How much more can this take hold in our finite flesh?
p: As Peter reminds us;
scripture: 2 Peter 3:8-9 | mark: The Lord is not slack concerning his promise

--- They that wait upon the Lord
kicker: Psalm 13 · Consider and hear me
scripture: Psalm 13:3-4 | mark: Consider and hear me, O LORD my God
p: God calls upon His faithful to wait. And in that waiting, there is promise as well.
big: Isaiah 40:31 | mark: they that wait upon the LORD shall renew their strength

--- To temperance patience
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 13:3
p: In our patience, God gives us added strength. It is part of a ladder of virtue in the Christian life, elevating us to godliness.
scripture: 2 Peter 1:5-8 | mark: to temperance patience; and to patience godliness

--- He hath dealt bountifully with me
kicker: Psalm 13 · I have trusted in thy mercy
scripture: Psalm 13:5-6 | mark: because he hath dealt bountifully with me
p: In the end, our prayers for God to act, and reminders that He has promised to tend to our welfare, and bring us peace from our enemies must inevitably lead to our recognition of His mercies and blessings. Of His salvation.
p: Because, as we call out to Him, it is impossible to not remember the very reason we are trusting in Him, beginning with eternal life granted through Jesus. And with the heavenly perspective of God's grace, it is incredibly difficult to continue in complaint. *Because he hath dealt bountifully with* us. We've already received more than we could ever deserve.

--- My grace is sufficient for thee
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 13:5
scripture: 2 Corinthians 12:7-11 | mark: My grace is sufficient for thee
p: It's also good to remember that, if a prayer remains "unanswered" by our measure, it very well may be answered in His. That His grace is sufficient. We've already been spared, hell and death, and given the glory of eternal life in Christ.

--- Remember the Israelites
kicker: Application • Complaint
p: Remember the Israelites, who when delivered out of Egypt and bondage, complained incessantly in the wilderness. They complained about a lack of water. They complained about the daunting task of taking the land, despite God promising to drive the inhabitants out before them. They believed the bad spies report, and ignored Joshua and Caleb. They complained about manna, and they complained about quail. And God had to constantly remind them that He had delivered them in the first place.
p: Sometimes if we feel the need to complain to God, we should remember that he's delivered us in the first place. That His grace is sufficient. Reminding ourselves of what we're going through currently is nothing in comparison to the abundance we've already received.

--- The sufferings of this present time
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 13:6
scripture: Romans 8:14-18 | mark: the sufferings of this present time are not worthy to be compared with the glory which shall be revealed in us

--- Patience builds godliness
kicker: Application • Waiting
p: Keeping that eternal perspective gives us faith in the Lord's ability to deliver us. The suffering of this present time is nothing. Life is but a vapor.
p: So, having persistent prayer and letting God know how you feel while waiting for His perfect timing to occur is fine. But keep that perspective in mind. And understand that patience builds godliness. And everything to His glory, amen.

=== Psalm 14
--- Psalm 14 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 14
sub: The fool hath said in his heart, There is no God
refs: Psalm 14:1

--- There is none that doeth good
kicker: Psalm 14 · All have sinned
scripture: Psalm 14:1-3 | mark: The fool hath said in his heart, There is no God; there is none that doeth good, no, not one
p: The opening 3 verses of **Psalm 14** stand as the definitive and true statements of the doctrine that all men have sinned and fallen short of the glory of God. Quoted in part by Paul in **Romans 3:10–12**

--- There is none righteous
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 14:1–3
scripture: Romans 3:10-12 | mark: There is none righteous, no, not one
p: It serves as a universal appeal to the need for the gospel, and the reconciliation of fallen men with a holy God. There is none good but God. All have gone astray.
p: The opening verse is an even deeper truth, showing the self-deception that leads fools to claim there is no God. They may say this, but ultimately every man knows that God is, and the judge of all things.

--- They eat up my people as bread
kicker: Psalm 14 · The workers of iniquity
scripture: Psalm 14:4-6 | mark: who eat up my people as they eat bread, and call not upon the LORD; Ye have shamed the counsel of the poor
p: Like the scribes and Pharisees that Jesus preaches against in **Matthew 23**, these fools, the workers of iniquity, devour the poor. They eat up the people as bread.
p: They call not upon the Lord. They shame the counsel of the poor, they profane the hope of the righteous, they deny the Lord that bought them.

--- The salvation of Israel out of Zion
kicker: Psalm 14 · Jacob shall rejoice
big: Psalm 14:7 | mark: Oh that the salvation of Israel were come out of Zion!
p: Again, the eschatological application is clearly seen in the text, and the prophetic words of David's prayer is ultimately about Jesus.
p: He is the salvation come out of Zion, and at His coming, Jesus brings all the risen saints, death and Hell defeated.
p: This was the hope David had. And the hope that God's judgment and resurrection of the righteous and wrath to the wicked, was the refuge of the poor and the oppressed.
p: This is our future, rejoice.

=== Psalm 15
--- Psalm 15 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 15
sub: Who shall abide in thy tabernacle?
refs: Psalm 15:1

--- A life of separation
kicker: Psalm 15 · Who shall abide?
big: Psalm 15:1 | mark: who shall dwell in thy holy hill?
p: The Christian life is one of separation. Of leaving behind the things of this world, and the wickedness men do, separated unto His gospel, and proclaiming His truth.
p: Epistles in the New Testament show us examples of godly living. Admonishments against the behavior that the lost may do, but we should strive to reject even the company of those that behave as the heathen.
big: 1 Corinthians 5:11 | mark: not to keep company

--- Backbitings, whisperings
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 15:3
scripture: 2 Corinthians 12:19-21 | mark: backbitings, whisperings

--- He that backbiteth not
kicker: Psalm 15 · Speaketh the truth in his heart
scripture: Psalm 15:2-3 | mark: speaketh the truth in his heart; He that backbiteth not with his tongue
p: Here, David gives a similar refrain. Against backbiting, talking behind someone's back, railing and gossiping. Against evil done to your neighbor. The very traits David spends multiple Psalms praying imprecatory prayers against workers of iniquity for doing, should not be spoken of among God's children.

--- He that sweareth to his own hurt
kicker: Psalm 15 · A vile person is contemned
big: Psalm 15:4 | mark: He that sweareth to his own hurt, and changeth not
p: We should condemn vile persons, not partake in promoting them, or their pride in sin. We should honor and fear the Lord. In all our ways, acknowledging Him.
p: We should keep our vows and promises, especially when it is not to our favor. Where we suffer loss for our honesty's sake.

--- He shall never be moved
kicker: Psalm 15 · Usury
big: Psalm 15:5 | mark: He that doeth these things shall never be moved
p: We should avoid usury, and taking advantage of the poor and desperate. Instead, with gratitude to the Lord, lend freely. By our actions we establish ourselves in truth. Building upon the rock, by being doers of the word.
p: We shall do these things and shall never be moved.

--- The apple of God's eye
kicker: Application • The mind of a King
p: Imagine you are a King, and powerful among the people, yet your empathy and thoughts are for justice and the poor, widows, fatherless. Making your priorities about others.
p: David, far from being sinless, yet this was the mind within him. His power wasn't for him, but from God to help the helpless.
p: This is what made him the apple of God's eye. And it is the calling we are to answer, as princes of the Kingdom. The same instructions in humility and decency that Solomon, David's heir, taught in Proverbs. The same as the teachings and example of Christ Jesus in His ministry as a Servant.
p: May this be our starting position, every morning. In our prayers and supplication before the throne, seeking the benefit of others and the glorification of God.

